WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:06.000
We're living in a world right
now where we have more data

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.985
than we know what to do
with almost, right? And so,

00:00:08.985 --> 00:00:12.345
then we turn that corner
to what are those insights,

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:14.745
what are those specific tools,

00:00:14.745 --> 00:00:17.470
what's that specific leverage
that we can get to our

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.790
customers. And that's
really, I think,

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:22.910
what we wake up every day and
try to put that thinking cap on

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:24.910
and empathize with
the end golfer.

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:30.755
Try solve problems for them they
might not even know they have.

00:00:30.755 --> 00:00:33.470
There's been an explosion
of data. So data

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:35.710
on how different
golf balls fly,

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:40.350
data on how golfers
move around the course,

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:44.115
and then layer on top of
that, intelligent analytics

00:00:44.115 --> 00:00:47.235
on what does that data mean?

00:00:47.235 --> 00:00:50.320
And so we now need
to figure out how to

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.400
take that data, take
those measurements, analyze it,

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.520
and and ultimately extract
insights to help people play

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.625
better golf, enjoy
the game more.

00:00:59.625 --> 00:01:02.585
And that requires
tools and structures

00:01:02.585 --> 00:01:06.985
to help us not only process
and and and analyze that

00:01:06.985 --> 00:01:10.620
data but then also deliver it
to the end user and and help

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.340
the customer understand and
leverage those insights that

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.325
we're getting from these
new measurements and new technologies.

00:01:16.325 --> 00:01:19.045
It's amazing what smart people
can accomplish with with good

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:22.405
tools. But then being
able to take that

00:01:22.405 --> 00:01:24.820
and quickly turn it
into a production-grade

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:25.940
commercially-viable

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:30.180
web app and get that out to public
was that was a big win. And since then,

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.435
we've taken that momentum and
repeated it several more times.

00:01:34.435 --> 00:01:37.635
I think we're just at the very
beginning of being able to

00:01:37.635 --> 00:01:41.015
bring this collection of
tools to the everyday golfer.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:50.600
So I was introduced to PING
through a colleague who is a a

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:51.965
designer here,

00:01:51.965 --> 00:01:54.125
and I got in and
started working part-time in the

00:01:54.125 --> 00:01:56.365
manufacturing engineering group, and
I just love the

00:01:56.365 --> 00:01:58.205
culture. I love
solving problems.

00:01:58.205 --> 00:02:00.850
I'd never envisioned that I
would use my engineering degree

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.450
to even get into golf design.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:05.410
So it's been a very fun journey
because I've been able to

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:08.795
invest time in my game, follow
my passion, get better at golf,

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:10.555
join the PGA of America,

00:02:10.555 --> 00:02:13.675
and that's opened up some
doors to play competitive tournament

00:02:13.675 --> 00:02:17.190
golf. But when that door opened
to get in here, I loved it.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:20.150
I loved the culture, I
loved learning about all the

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:24.070
unknowns, all the golf
physics that was there,

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:25.435
and the curiosity.

00:02:25.435 --> 00:02:27.995
I was an R and D engineer.
He was a designer.

00:02:27.995 --> 00:02:31.435
He always has just been
really consumed with what the customer

00:02:31.435 --> 00:02:33.810
needs. How do I help
somebody play the game more?

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:36.450
And I was more on the
research side trying to help answer the

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:40.690
questions that he was
posing. I think that culture

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:44.535
of being so curious about how
things work and then being able

00:02:44.535 --> 00:02:48.295
to kind of take ownership
to try to solve a problem,

00:02:48.295 --> 00:02:51.255
and then ultimately turn
it into a solution for the

00:02:51.255 --> 00:02:53.420
customer, right?

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:54.780
And I love that.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.420
As I started working
here at PING,

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.285
I started playing golf more
and more, and that became

00:03:00.285 --> 00:03:04.285
more of a passion for me even
outside of my time here on

00:03:04.285 --> 00:03:06.365
campus doing the
engineering things.

00:03:06.365 --> 00:03:08.580
And it's really
helped me understand

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:13.460
the elements of the game that
are a challenge, the pain

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:16.180
of a player trying to get
better and it's really helped

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:20.135
me understand how to ask
the right research questions and

00:03:20.135 --> 00:03:23.255
tackle the right areas to
help make golf equipment better.

00:03:23.255 --> 00:03:25.815
A lot of new people got
introduced to golf during the

00:03:25.815 --> 00:03:30.680
past few years. And so there's
kind of a younger generation, a

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:33.480
network of golfers that
previously weren't introduced

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.575
to the game or out there,
And so we've tried to just really,

00:03:36.575 --> 00:03:38.575
both from a product standpoint,

00:03:38.575 --> 00:03:41.055
from a custom
fitting standpoint,

00:03:41.055 --> 00:03:43.055
try to continue that momentum.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:54.935
PING was founded by
a frustrated engineer, Karsten Solheim.

00:03:54.935 --> 00:03:58.455
He founded the company because
he looked at how equipment was

00:03:58.455 --> 00:04:00.150
designed and said, hey,

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:03.590
I could bring my engineering
background and make the game

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:06.630
way easier through
some fundamental, you know,

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:08.085
physics principles.

00:04:08.085 --> 00:04:11.765
And it's still kind of in the
DNA of PING in what we do.

00:04:11.765 --> 00:04:14.965
Every company develops
little centers of excellence;

00:04:14.965 --> 00:04:19.280
groups of one or two folks who
who try to solve a problem the

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:21.920
best way they can or the
the best way they knew how.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:24.240
And one of those that
had developed at PING,

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.365
that I learned about,

00:04:25.365 --> 00:04:29.045
was a group in engineering
that we're using the R programming

00:04:29.045 --> 00:04:33.685
language to help visualize
and share insights and information

00:04:33.685 --> 00:04:37.430
about golf data and
how we do business.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:38.150
Initially,

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:41.430
we started looking at Shiny
apps to help just visualize and

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:46.365
quickly draw insights from data
that we acquired on our range

00:04:46.365 --> 00:04:50.320
here, through player testing,
from our motion cap system.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:55.760
Shiny apps at PING really are
where the first value from R

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:57.335
came out. That's that's Dr.

00:04:57.335 --> 00:05:00.215
Henriksen and and Marty and
their teams who are the clever

00:05:00.215 --> 00:05:01.415
folks behind those.

00:05:01.415 --> 00:05:03.735
A lot of these physics-based

00:05:03.735 --> 00:05:06.170
data-driven models
that we were developing

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:10.890
we were developing them
using Posit R Studio.

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:13.610
Then using like the
Shiny framework,

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:17.565
developing these dashboards for our
engineers to then use these

00:05:17.565 --> 00:05:20.525
models and use the data we
get from player testing and motion

00:05:20.525 --> 00:05:23.640
capture testing to
design equipment.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:25.960
And so we went to Doug
and we're like, hey,

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:27.720
we have these models.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.760
We're using this Shiny
framework to develop these

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:33.735
Shiny dashboards and apps
to kind of beta test some

00:05:33.735 --> 00:05:36.615
different ideas we have for
for tools that would help both

00:05:36.615 --> 00:05:39.200
engineers as well
as the end consumer.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:39.520
Dr.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:42.960
Hendriksen and his team had
identified R as something they

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:44.640
wanted to work on and
something they wanted to do,

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.365
and they had built a handful
of of Shiny applications.

00:05:48.365 --> 00:05:51.405
But they did it without the
support of IT and without the

00:05:51.405 --> 00:05:54.340
support of the larger
corporate digital strategy.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:56.340
Because they had to, they
were solving a problem,

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:58.180
they they were innovating.So

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:00.955
I I was glad to be able
to to come in and say,

00:06:00.955 --> 00:06:03.035
that's fantastic, but
that doesn't scale,

00:06:03.035 --> 00:06:05.275
let's try some some
other techniques.

00:06:05.275 --> 00:06:09.660
And that's where Posit
Connect came in. As not only

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:12.860
providing us the ability to
publish these tools that we're

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:17.315
developing, but also host
APIs, and help us move

00:06:17.315 --> 00:06:20.275
toward consumer-facing
applications.

00:06:20.275 --> 00:06:22.755
Where we can then
build out a front-end

00:06:22.755 --> 00:06:26.290
and then hook that into
these APIs and Posit Connect.

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:29.330
The big proof of value came
the first time that I convinced

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:33.665
them to, instead of creating a
Shiny app, create a Plumber API.

00:06:33.665 --> 00:06:37.425
And then can I share that
Plumber API with a team within

00:06:37.425 --> 00:06:40.225
PING who can build a web
interface around it and publish

00:06:40.225 --> 00:06:43.340
that to the public? And
that that really was the, like,

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:44.860
okay, this is gonna work.

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:46.860
Instead of rebuilding

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:49.180
all of our algorithms
and fundamental modeling,

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:54.355
we're able to use APIs
that drive that front-end that our

00:06:54.355 --> 00:06:57.075
customers experience without
having to rebuild our

00:06:57.075 --> 00:07:00.670
prototype. We just built our
front-end right on top of it.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:04.910
Posit Connect gives a
tool set to program real

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:08.035
JSON-based APIs - that's fundamental.
If you can't do that,

00:07:08.035 --> 00:07:12.115
you're going to have a really
hard time working with any other system.

00:07:12.115 --> 00:07:15.450
How it works is that we
take those models and then we're

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:19.530
able to create in that
logic and develop some APIs,

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:22.995
and and through the
Plumber framework and through Connect,

00:07:22.995 --> 00:07:26.835
host those, and then
provide this foundation

00:07:26.835 --> 00:07:29.875
for other tools
to be developed.

00:07:29.875 --> 00:07:33.410
Shiny and Posit Connect
give us that ability to,

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:37.170
in a careful way, in a
protected way, with user access

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:40.245
controls and a reliable
deployment process,

00:07:40.245 --> 00:07:43.925
it gives us that ability to
to have their projects be shared

00:07:43.925 --> 00:07:47.660
around the company in a
in a controlled, safe, responsible way.

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:51.740
What started with me kind of
playing with it and and prototyping,

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:55.820
quickly evolved into a number
of engineers wanting to get

00:07:55.820 --> 00:07:58.745
their hands dirty, make
their own dashboards,

00:07:58.745 --> 00:08:01.865
do their own data analysis,
and exploded really quick.

00:08:01.865 --> 00:08:06.400
To me, what Posit Connect did
for us was was really brought

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:10.000
that that likelihood of getting
value from the investment and

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:13.745
and the time and the expertise
in data science. That

00:08:13.745 --> 00:08:16.865
shortened that life cycle a lot to
Can we can we get this out?

00:08:16.865 --> 00:08:19.745
Can we can we build a
consumer-facing product that

00:08:19.745 --> 00:08:23.660
has commercial value quickly
and cost-efficiently and and

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:25.020
try to see if it's worth it?

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:27.820
And I'm happy to say that
the solution we've got in place

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:30.325
right now lets us experiment
really, really fast.

00:08:30.325 --> 00:08:35.045
It's a lot of fun to be
able to kinda help build this ecosystem

00:08:35.045 --> 00:08:36.005
around data.

00:08:36.005 --> 00:08:39.045
Not only data when you're
hitting balls on a driving

00:08:39.045 --> 00:08:42.810
range or there when you're in
clubs or hitting in a simulator

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:44.570
bay. But more importantly,

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:48.525
what is your data on the
golf course while you're playing golf?

00:08:48.525 --> 00:08:51.325
And how can you tie those
things together and make better

00:08:51.325 --> 00:08:52.925
product decisions,

00:08:52.925 --> 00:08:54.285
custom-fitting decisions?

00:08:54.285 --> 00:08:56.900
Where should you be
practicing your game?

00:08:56.900 --> 00:09:00.100
Where you're gonna get the most
leverage from your practice in your training?

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:03.300
And it's still early for us
to make those tools available to a

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:05.255
lot of golfers at scale.

00:09:05.255 --> 00:09:07.255
Looking at at

00:09:07.255 --> 00:09:11.015
Posit and specifically Posit
Connect was not a short-term

00:09:11.015 --> 00:09:15.320
solution. I I saw it
as a strategic component of a

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:19.320
long-term vision for for
digital technology at PING.

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:22.360
If it worked out, which I'm
I'm really glad to say it did.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:24.295
I don't see that runway ending.

00:09:24.295 --> 00:09:27.355
That that's a foundational component
to how we continue innovating.

